\label{lem:twomodfour-upper}
Let $N=(p,r,q)\in U$, where
$p\equiv q\equiv4\pmod{16}$ and $r\equiv2\pmod4$.  Then every Jordan \(D(N)\)-set has at most five elements.

\label{prop:exceptional-six}
Let $N=(-s^2,r,-t^2)$, where $s,t\in\{1,2\}$, $r\in\mathbb Z$. 
If \(s\ne t\) or \(r\ne0\), every Jordan \(D(N)\)-set has at most six elements. If \(s=t\) and \(r=0\), there is an infinite Jordan \(D(N)\)-set consisting of nonsingular matrices.
